# Public env vars (NEXT_PUBLIC_* are inlined into the build).
# Copy to .env.local for local testing; set in Cloudflare Pages → Settings →
# Variables and Secrets for production builds.

# GA4 measurement ID. Must match the format G-XXXXXXXXXX.
# Leave empty to disable GA4 entirely (component renders nothing).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-0FCGXJHMMY

# Cookiebot Domain Group ID (UUID). Leave empty to disable the consent
# banner — useful for local dev when you don't want the banner in the way.
# NOTE: since 2026-08-08 the banner really does gate Google and Meta. Leaving
# this empty disables Cookiebot, which means the Consent Mode default in
# components/analytics/consent-default.tsx is never updated: GA4 then stays
# denied (no _ga cookie) and the Meta Pixel never loads. That is the intended
# fail-closed behaviour, not a misconfiguration.
NEXT_PUBLIC_COOKIEBOT_CBID=

# PostHog (product analytics, EU cloud). NEXT_PUBLIC_* → inlined into the
# client bundle (PostHog is browser-only here; static export has no server
# runtime). Project 143695, shared across the Let's Dog ecosystem. The key is
# a public client-side ingestion key (safe to commit, like the GA4 ID above).
# Leave KEY empty to disable PostHog (provider renders children, skips init).
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_Uxz55z5X0tMh6NHp8WWF3lD6oy2G3Xf0NiOZrrBJgFq
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com

# Meta (Facebook) Pixel id — 15-16 digits. Public by nature (it ships in the
# page source), like the GA4 id above. Leave empty to disable the pixel
# entirely (the component renders nothing).
# MUST be set in Cloudflare Pages -> Settings -> Variables and Secrets for BOTH
# Production AND Preview as a PLAINTEXT variable — NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at
# build time, so an unset variable means the pixel silently never renders.
# Does not load until Cookiebot grants marketing consent, and stops on
# withdrawal (see the header comment in components/analytics/meta-pixel.tsx).
#
# Dataset "Letsdog A team", in use since 2026-08-11 (platform loop decision
# D-99). The platform sends Purchase into this SAME dataset over the Conversions
# API, so this value has to stay in step with it: a funnel split across two
# datasets reports no purchases at all, on either one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=958837033882897

# Cloudflare Pages reads NODE_VERSION from env to pin the build container's
# Node version. Set to 20 in Pages production env, not committed here.
# NODE_VERSION=20

# --- Contact form (Cloudflare Pages Function: functions/api/contact.ts) ---
# Postmark Server API token. SERVER-SIDE SECRET — do NOT prefix with
# NEXT_PUBLIC (that would inline it into the client bundle). Set it in
# Cloudflare Pages -> Settings -> Variables and Secrets, scoped to BOTH
# Production AND Preview. Without it, /api/contact returns HTTP 500.
# POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=

# Optional overrides (the Function falls back to these defaults):
# CONTACT_TO=support@letsdog.nl
# CONTACT_FROM=noreply@letsdog.nl   # must be a Postmark-verified sender
#
# Cloudflare Turnstile (anti-abuse on the form). Create a Managed widget in the
# Cloudflare dashboard with hostnames letsdog.nl + www.letsdog.nl +
# website-letsdog.pages.dev. In Pages -> Variables and Secrets, PRODUCTION scope:
#   - NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY = the public Site Key, as a PLAINTEXT variable
#     (must be inlined into the client bundle at build; do NOT store it as a Secret).
#   - TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY = the Secret Key, as an encrypted SECRET (server-only;
#     do NOT prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC).
# Leave the Preview scope UNSET: branch previews fall back to Cloudflare's
# always-pass TEST keys (the real keys would fail on <branch>.pages.dev hosts,
# which aren't in the widget's hostname list). On a production host a missing
# secret fails closed (the Function returns 500) rather than silently open.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
